 { Sergei Zhigalko – Alexander Rakhmanov, Moscow 2011 Learning objective: This game is an example of White’s f4-f5 break and the resulting kingside attack. } 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 8. Qd2 b5 9. a3 Bb7 10. Bd3 Be7 11. O-O { Both players have closely followed the theory, and at this point we begin the middlegame phase. } Qc7 ( { Advisable was } 11... O-O 12. Qf2 f6 $1 { disputing White’s central control as in the game Gao – Gomez, Mashhad 2011. } ) 
12. dxc5 ( { Another typical move is } 12. Qf2 $5 { forcing Black to define the centre with } cxd4 ( { or } 12. dxc5 c4 $5 ) 
13. Nxd4 Nxd4 14. Bxd4 O-O 15. b4 $5 { ⩲ which is similar to the game. } ) 
Nxc5 13. b4 $1 { This is a good positional decision, driving the knight away and fixing the queenside structure on a6 and b5. In some cases pushing b2-b4 could be weakening, especially to the c2-pawn, but here there is nothing to worry about, as White’s position is rock solid. } Nd7 ( { If } 13... Nxd3 14. cxd3 O-O 15. Rac1 Rfc8 16. Qf2 $14 { White has good control of the dark squares, while the bad bishop on b7 is really bad. PH: It's telling that in the position after } 14. cxd3 { the computer says that Black should give up a pawn with } d4 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 15. Bxd4 O-O { . At least the b7-bishop has an open diagonal... Not surprisingly, in the position after } 14. cxd3 O-O { , instead of } 15. Rac1 { it wants to play } 15. Nd4 $1 Nxd4 16. Bxd4 { , preventing Black from sacrificing with ...d4. } ) 
14. Ne2 { A natural move, heading towards d4, but probably not the most precise. } ( { Black experiences some difficulties after } 14. Qf2 $1 { planning to proceed with Ne2 and Ned4 and then a kingside attack, but having first prevented the manoeuvre ...Nb6-c4 . Now a possible reaction is: } f6 $5 ( { the tempting } 14. Ne2 Nxb4 $2 15. axb4 Qxc3 { fails due to } 16. f5 $1 { ± when White obtains a strong attack. } ) 
 ( { while } 14. Ne2 O-O 15. Ne2 $14 { is similar to the game } ) 
15. Nd4 Nxd4 16. Bxd4 O-O 17. Qh4 ( 17. Qe3 $5 { ⩲ } ) 
g6 18. Qh3 $1 f5 19. g4 $5 { ⩲ } ) 
Nb6 15. Ned4 Nc4 16. Qe1 Nxe3 $2 { This is a very poor decision in my opinion. Black clarifies the situation in the centre and gives White a free hand to carry out a kingside attack with f4-f5. } ( { The right continuation was } 16... O-O 17. g4 Nxd4 18. Bxd4 a5 { when Black has good queenside counterplay since } 19. f5 { is met by } exf5 20. gxf5 f6 $1 { with an unclear position. PH: The computer disagrees with Flores Rios' evaluation here, and says that after } O-O 17. Nxc6 Bxc6 18. Bd4 { White is more or less winning because the c6-bishop is out of the game and White can freely start an attack on the kingside. } ) 
17. Qxe3 { This is a good moment to assess the position. White is better for the following reasons: 1. He has undisputed control of the d4-square. 2. Black’s bad bishop is completely locked in by his pawns. 3. White may create a kingside attack, while Black cannot advance on the queenside. 4. The pressure on e6 prevents Black from one of his typical plans – the break ...f7-f6. } g6 18. c3 ( { Launching an attack with } 18. g4 { is a little premature due to } h5 $1 19. h3 Nxd4 20. Nxd4 hxg4 21. hxg4 Rh4 { with an unclear position. } ) 
O-O $6 { Black is basically inviting White to begin his kingside attack, as he has probably underestimated how strong it will actually be. } ( { It was probably better to play } 18... h5 $5 { delaying the kingside attack, but now White can direct his attention to the queenside with } 19. a4 $14 { or play } 19. g3 $5 { to slowly build a kingside attack after h2-h3, g3-g4, etc. } ) 
19. g4 { Everything is ready to continue with f4-f5. } Nxd4 20. Nxd4 $2 { This move is conceptually correct, as the knight and rook support the break f4-f5. Unfortunately, it misses a stronger continuation, and allows a strong reply, as we shall see. } ( { Stronger was } 20. cxd4 $1 { when Black has nothing better than } f5 { to prevent f4-f5 } ( { if } 20. Nxd4 Rfc8 21. f5 Qc3 22. h4 $1 { White gains a crushing attack, for example } Bf8 23. fxe6 fxe6 24. h5 Bh6 25. Qxh6 Qxd3 26. hxg6 Qxg6 27. Qxg6 hxg6 28. Ng5 ) 
 { but after } 21. exf6 Rxf6 22. Ne5 $16 { White has a stable positional advantage, which is similar to the game Areshchenko – Vovk from the previous chapter. } ) 
Qb6 $2 { The decisive mistake. } ( 20... Kh8 { would have allowed Black to meet } 21. f5 { with the strong } gxf5 22. gxf5 Bg5 $1 { with a rather unclear position. } ) 
 ( { But instead, after } 20... Kh8 { White has the simple } 21. Rac1 { protecting the c3-pawn and preparing an attack with h2-h4-h5. Black does not have much to do, for example } Rac8 22. h4 Rfe8 ( 22... Bxh4 $2 23. g5 $18 ) 
23. h5 Bf8 24. Kg2 Bg7 25. Rh1 $16 { with a crushing attack. } ) 
 ( { It was necessary to divert the attention away from the kingside attack with } 20... Qxc3 $1 { and now after: } 21. Rac1 ( { or the unclear } 20... Qb6 20. Kh1 $5 { followed by f4-f5 to insist on a kingside attack. } ) 
 ( { But not } 20... Qb6 20. f5 $2 { due to } Bg5 $1 { winning a piece } ) 
 ( { PH: Here a beautiful line is } 20... Qb6 20. Rfc1 Qb2 22. Bxg6 { , threatening to trap the queen with Rcb1, when } Rfc8 $3 { is the only move that keeps Black in the game. } ) 
Qxa3 22. Rc7 { Winning a piece for three pawns. } Bxb4 23. Nc2 Qa5 24. Rxb7 Bd2 $5 { The position is rather unclear, but White retains a plus due to his attacking prospects. } ) 
21. f5 $1 { White achieves the typical f4-f5 break with a crushing attack. } exf5 ( 21... Rfc8 { doesn’t help because of: } 22. fxg6 fxg6 23. Bxg6 $1 { This might be what Black missed. } hxg6 24. Qh6 { White is winning after the forcing line } Rxc3 25. Qxg6 Kh8 26. Qh5 Kg8 27. Qf7 Kh8 28. Rad1 $1 { followed by Qxe7 or Qh5 and Rf7 . } ) 
22. gxf5 gxf5 23. Kh1 Kh8 ( { White is also winning after } 23... Bc8 24. Bxf5 Bxf5 25. Rxf5 Kh8 26. e6 $1 f6 ( { or } 26... fxe6 27. Qe5 ) 
27. Rh5 { with forced mate. For example: } Rg8 28. Rxh7 $1 Kxh7 29. Qh3 Kg7 30. Rg1 Kf8 31. Qh6 Ke8 32. Rxg8 Bf8 33. Qxf8 ) 
24. Rxf5 f6 25. e6 Rg8 26. Rh5 { Black resigns due to the imminent checkmate. Final remarks 1. Although White’s move 13. b4 seems like a positional mistake (it creates a weak c4-square, and turns c2 into a backward pawn) this move is thematic in this position and it works well to cover the c5-square and fix Black’s queenside structure. This prevents Black from playing ...b5-b4 and ...a6-a5, thus keeping Black’s bishop on b7 locked out of the game. 2. Black’s biggest mistake was 16...Nxe3 which allowed White some extra support for his e5-pawn, to later push f4-f5 optimally. 3. The reader should remember Black’s reaction ...exf5 followed by ...f6! to stop White’s attack in the note 16...O-O. 4. Once White achieves the f4-f5 break under optimal conditions, there is essentially nothing Black can do to prevent a deadly mating attack. A reason for White’s success in this game was his control of the d4-square and Black’s inability to use his bishop on b7. In practical terms, White had an extra piece for the attack, and thus it comes as no surprise that his attack was a success. } 
 1-0

