 { Sergey Karjakin – Magnus Carlsen, Wijk aan Zee 2010 Learning objective: This game is an example of Black’s ...f7-f6 break. Note how White’s attacking chances die out after it. } 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Be2 a6 10. O-O b5 { So far both players have been following the theory, and now Karjakin goes into a low-scoring sideline: } 11. Kh1 ( { A more common line is } 11. a3 Qb6 12. Nd1 a5 13. c3 ( { or } 13. Nf2 Ba6 14. c3 b4 { when Black manages to trade light-squared bishops } ) 
a4 { with level chances. White can carry out a typical kingside attack, while Black can play with ...Na5-b3 or ...b5-b4 . } ) 
Qc7 { We are already out of theory and the position is balanced. } 12. a3 Bb7 13. Rad1 Rac8 14. Qe1 { Intending Qg3 (or Qh4 if possible) to direct an attack against Black’s king. I personally would not recommend this here since White does not have firm control of the centre yet. } ( { Another option was } 14. dxc5 $5 Nxc5 15. Qe1 { followed by Qf2 , heading for a positional fight. } ) 
 ( { PH: After } 14. dxc5 { the computer suggests } Bxc5 15. Bxc5 Nxc5 { . It might look weird, exchanging a piece that could control the d4-square, but the point is revealed after } 16. Qe3 Qb6 17. Bd3 h6 18. Ne2 d4 $1 { - again a pawn sacrifice to free Black's pieces. Play might continue } 19. Nexd4 Nxd4 20. Nxd4 Na4 { with ...Rfd8 to come and good compensation. } ) 
cxd4 { Of course, White is preparing a flank attack, therefore Black reacts in the centre. } 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 16. Bxd4 Bc5 17. Qh4 $2 { This is overly optimistic; now White’s centre will suffer. } ( { It was not too late to accept the mistake and settle for an equal position with } 17. Qd2 Bxd4 18. Qxd4 { and then } Qc5 ( { or } 18. Qh4 f6 $5 ) 
 ) 
Bxd4 18. Rxd4 f6 $1 { A timely reaction; White’s kingside plans suddenly come to a stop and White’s centre becomes rather fragile. } 19. Bd3 $6 { This move only aggravates White’s problems, as the rook is awkwardly trapped on d4. } ( { Better was } 19. exf6 Rxf6 20. Qe1 Rcf8 { although Black is better since the f4-pawn is much weaker than the e6-pawn, and the bishop on b7 is coming into the game soon, for example: } 21. Bg4 Qb6 22. Rd1 d4 $15 ) 
h6 20. exf6 ( { If } 20. Qg4 Nc5 $1 ( { but not } 20. exf6 f5 $2 { relieving the central tension, and solving White’s problems } ) 
 { there is nothing better than } 21. exf6 Rxf6 $17 { which is similar to the game. } ) 
Rxf6 $17 { A very important moment in the game. Black has found a good time to break the pawn chain with ...f7-f6, and the f4-pawn has become a serious weakness. From a static perspective, White’s pawn structure is superior; in fact Black has many weaknesses. However, White does not have the time or the pieces to take advantage of those weaknesses, and the threat of ...e6-e5 is very strong. White’s pieces lack coordination and there is not even time to attack the typically weak e6-pawn. } 21. f5 { Attempting to get away from the trouble through tactical means, but Black is better prepared for these complications. } ( { If } 21. Qe1 Rcf8 22. g3 $2 { hoping to hold the position, then } Qc5 { taking advantage of the awkwardly-placed rook. } 23. Rb4 e5 $1 { -+ And White’s centre collapses. Notice the crucial role Black’s ‘bad’ bishop plays in this attack. } ) 
Rcf8 22. Rg1 { There is nothing better. } ( { Instead } 22. Re1 Nc5 23. fxe6 $2 { loses to: } Nxd3 24. Rxd3 Rf1 25. Rxf1 Rxf1 ) 
Nc5 $1 { Threatening ...Nxd3 and ...Rxf5 winning. } 23. fxe6 Nxe6 24. Rg4 $2 { The rook is very awkwardly located on g4. } ( { Better was } 24. Nxd5 { but Carlsen was ready to reply } Bxd5 25. Rxd5 Nf4 26. Rd4 Qc5 $1 { and White must give up the exchange with } 27. Rxf4 ( { since } 27. Re4 $4 { loses on the spot due to } Nxd3 28. cxd3 Qxg1 $1 29. Kxg1 Rf1 ) 
Rxf4 $17 { when White has meagre drawing chances. } ) 
Nf4 { Black is essentially winning already, due to his activity and the threat of ...d5-d4 bringing the bishop decisively into the game. } 25. Qg3 ( 25. Ne2 { doesn’t help due to } Nxd3 26. cxd3 Qe7 $1 { with a winning attack: } 27. Nd4 ( { but not } 27. Qe1 $2 Re6 $19 ) 
Qe3 { The problem is White’s rook being stuck on g4; the threat is ...Qxg1 , and now } 28. Nf3 { is met by: } d4 $1 { The bad bishop arrives to decide the game: } 29. h3 ( { or } 29. Nxd4 $2 Qxg1 30. Kxg1 Rf1 ) 
Rxf3 30. gxf3 Bxf3 $19 ) 
Qe7 $1 { As Carlsen explains in his analysis, the point of this move is to deprive the knight on c3 of a good square after ...d4. White is pretty much defenceless. } 26. Rxf4 { Giving up the exchange after all; there was nothing better. } ( { If White simply waits with } 26. h3 { then Carlsen offers the variation: } d4 27. Re1 ( { or } 27. Ne2 Nxe2 28. Bxe2 Bc8 $1 { winning an exchange } ) 
 ( { or } 27. Nd1 Bc8 { also winning an exchange } ) 
Qxe1 $1 28. Qxe1 Nxd3 { Winning a decisive amount of material, for example: } 29. Qe7 Nf2 30. Kg1 Nxg4 31. hxg4 dxc3 $19 ) 
Rxf4 $19 { Black is an exchange up for no compensation. All he needs is to bring his bishop into the game in order to finish it off. } 27. Ne2 Rf1 28. Nd4 ( { If } 28. c3 { , hoping to create a fortress with Nd4 , then } Rxg1 29. Kxg1 Re8 { threatening ...Qe3 trading queens. } 30. Kf2 d4 $1 { The bishop enters the fray ! } 31. cxd4 Be4 { And Black is winning. } ) 
Rxg1+ 29. Kxg1 Re8 { Now the queen trade is unavoidable. } 30. h4 Qe1+ 31. Kh2 Qxg3+ 32. Kxg3 Kf7 { The endgame is winning, although the knight on d4 helps White hold the position for a while. The game finished: } 33. Kf2 Kf6 34. g3 Bc8 35. c3 Bg4 36. Bc2 g5 37. hxg5+ hxg5 38. Bb3 Ke5 39. Bc2 Rf8+ 40. Kg2 Bd7 41. Nf3+ Kf6 42. Bb3 g4 43. Nd4 Ke5 { White cannot prevent the creation of weaknesses in his camp with ...a6-a5 and ...b5-b4. } 44. Bc2 a5 45. Bd1 ( { Or } 45. Bd3 b4 46. axb4 axb4 47. Ne2 bxc3 48. bxc3 Bf5 49. Bxf5 Rxf5 { followed by ...Ke4-d3 . } ) 
Ke4 { White resigns, as the king invasion decides the game. Final remarks 1. The message of this game is that White needs a solid centre in order to successfully execute a kingside attack. 2. The break ...f7-f6 can sometimes be weakening for the e6-pawn, but it is a good method to distract attention from White’s attack, and it can even gain good counterplay if White is not prepared for this break. } 
 0-1

