 { Loek van Wely – Sergey Karjakin, Wijk aan Zee 2012 Learning objective: This game showcases the typical battle between the strong knight and the bad light-squared bishop, but with reversed colours! So it is actually White who has a bad dark-squared bishop. } 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e5 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. d3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e3 Be7 7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O Be6 9. a3 a5 10. Qc2 f5 { We have a position that is typical of a Scheveningen Sicilian with reversed colours, where the main plan is the central break d3-d4. } 11. Na4 Qd6 12. Rd1 Rad8 13. d4 e4 14. Nd2 { And Black has reached the position of interest under good conditions. This is similar to the game Zhigalko – Rakhmanov. Black has firm control of the d5-square while White’s bad bishop is still trapped on c1. } Bf7 15. Nc3 Qh6 { The queen begins to eye the kingside. } 16. Nxd5 Bxd5 17. Bc4 Kh8 18. Bxd5 Rxd5 { The last few moves, although logical, have only accentuated White’s positional problem: there is no clear way to bring his bishop into the game. } 19. Nf1 Rd6 { A potential idea is to play ...Rg6 with kingside threats. } ( { An interesting alternative was } 19... Rd7 $5 { intending to follow up with ...Bd6 and ...Ne7 immediately. } ) 
20. b4 ( { The move } 20. d5 $2 { loses a pawn after } Rfd8 $1 { . } ) 
axb4 21. axb4 Rd7 22. Bd2 $2 { This bishop will not find a way into the game again. } ( { If } 22. b5 { then simply } Nb4 23. Qb3 Nd3 $15 { when the knight on d3 is quite disturbing. } ) 
 ( { The right move was } 22. Ba3 $1 { attempting to trade off dark-squared bishops, and now } b5 $5 { as suggested by GM Marin, yields an approximately balanced game after } 23. Ng3 Qe6 24. Rac1 { since Black’s queenside is somewhat weakened, which compensates for White’s bad bishop. } ) 
Bd6 $1 { ⩱ Protecting the c7-pawn and clearing the way for the knight manoeuvre ...Ne7-d5. } ( { PH: Note that if } 22... Bxb4 { , then } 23. d5 $1 { gives White a lot of play. } ) 
23. b5 Ne7 24. Ra7 b6 { White’s queenside play runs out of fuel; there are no weaknesses to attack. } 25. Rda1 ( { If } 25. Qc4 { then Black continues with his plan of } Nd5 $1 { with a good position, since } 26. Qxd5 $2 { loses to } Bxh2 { . } ) 
Qe6 26. Ra8 ( { PH: Here Van Wely starts drifting. The regrouping } 26. Bc1 $1 Nd5 27. Ba3 $1 { , to exchange off Black's key bishop, would lead to equal play. } ) 
Rdd8 27. Rxd8 Rxd8 28. Ng3 $6 { This natural-looking move allows Black to clarify the situation after: } Bxg3 $1 29. hxg3 Nd5 $17 { This is a very instructive position to study. My computer evaluates it as ‘0.00’ (complete equality) , but this is certainly not the case. Black has a strategically-won position due to the difference between the strong knight on d5, and the rather useless bishop on d2. White’s queenside play cannot produce anything, while Black has the opportunity to create a powerful attack on the kingside, as we will see in the rest of this game. } 30. Ra7 h6 31. Qa2 Qe8 { Preventing Ra8, thus keeping the rooks on the board. } 32. Qc4 Qh5 { Threatening ...Qd1 winning the bishop. } 33. Ra1 Kh7 34. Bb4 Qg6 35. Be1 h5 { Black has slowly improved his position, and now the attack begins. } 36. Rc1 h4 { This is a very nice way of opening the position. It is only now that my computer begins to understand in how much trouble White really is. } ( { Another strong alternative was } 36... Qg4 $5 { preparing the break a little further, and now } 37. Rc2 h4 ( { or } 37... Rd6 $5 ) 
38. gxh4 Qxh4 39. Ra2 Qg4 { when Black can create a powerful attack with ...f5-f4 or ...Rd6-g6 . } ) 
37. gxh4 f4 { The break ...f5-f4 is strong, just like f4-f5 was strong in Zhigalko – Rakhmanov from earlier in this chapter. } 38. Qe2 ( { If } 38. exf4 Nxf4 39. Qf1 ( { or } 39. g3 Rxd4 $1 { ∓ } ) 
Rxd4 { Black regains the pawn favourably, since } 40. Rxc7 $2 { loses on the spot to } Rd1 { threatening ...Nd3 or ...Rxe1 . } ) 
 ( { Another option was: } 38. Bd2 f3 39. g3 Qf5 40. Kh2 { The only move but now Black gains a powerful attack. } ( { The natural-looking } 40. Ra1 { loses quickly after } g5 $1 41. hxg5 Rh8 { followed by ...Kg6 and ...Qh3 with a winning attack. } ) 
Rh8 { Threatening ...g7-g5 . } 41. Rh1 Ra8 42. Rc1 Nf6 $1 { This is the key move; the threat of ...Ng4xf2 is extremely powerful, and White’s position is already near collapse, for example: } 43. Qxc7 $2 ( { Or } 43. Rf1 Ra3 44. Bc3 Nd5 45. Bd2 Rd3 46. Bc1 Qd7 $17 { and the b5-pawn is probably lost, while ...g7-g5 continues to be a threat. } ) 
Ra2 44. Rc2 Ng4 45. Kg1 Nxf2 $1 46. Kxf2 ( 46. Rxa2 Qh3 { and wins. } ) 
Qh3 { With unstoppable mate. } ) 
f3 39. Qf1 Qg4 40. g3 g5 $1 { This is a powerful mating threat. White has nothing better than going into a lost endgame. } 41. Kh2 { The only move. } ( { Not } 41. hxg5 $2 { since after } Kg6 $1 { there is no way to prevent checkmate with ...Rh8 and ...Qh5-h1 , and if } 42. Rc6 { simply } Kxg5 { . } ) 
 ( { Similarly } 41. h5 $2 { loses in short order after } Qxh5 42. Rc6 Kg7 { followed by ...Rh8 and ...Qh1# . } ) 
gxh4 42. Qh3 Qxh3+ 43. Kxh3 hxg3 44. Kxg3 { The queens are off the board, but White’s big problem persists: his bishop remains useless on e1. } Rg8+ 45. Kh2 Rg2+ 46. Kh1 Rg6 47. Ra1 Rg8 { Black wanders around in search of the right winning plan; he can afford to take things slowly since White does not have any counterplay. } 48. Rc1 Kg6 49. Rc6+ ( { An amusing variation occurs after: } 49. Kg1 Kf5 50. Kf1 $2 { Hoping to get the king out of corner, but now } Rg2 51. Bd2 Nf6 $1 { and White’s position collapses since } 52. Rxc7 Ng4 { wins the f2-pawn to promote the f3-pawn, because } 53. Be1 $2 Nh2 { is the end. } ) 
Kf7 50. Rh6 ( { White cannot really hope for active play without the cooperation of his bishop, but the ”waiting strategy” was also hopeless. If White passively waits with } 50. Rc2 { then Black has many winning plans. For example, } Ke6 51. Rc1 Kd7 52. Rc2 Rg2 53. Rc1 Nf6 54. Rc2 Ng4 55. Rd2 Kc8 $1 { and White is in zugzwang since } 56. Ra2 { loses to } Nxe3 { . } ) 
Ra8 { Black has had a winning position for a while, but this is just the moment when White’s position collapses. } 51. Rh7+ Kg6 52. Rh4 Nf6 ( { Or: } 52... Ra1 $5 53. Rg4 Kf5 54. Rg1 Rb1 $19 ) 
53. Bb4 Kg5 54. Rf4 Ra1+ 55. Kh2 Ng4+ ( { White resigns. } 55... Ng4+ 55. Kh3 { loses to } Rh1 57. Kg3 Rg1 58. Kh3 Nxf2 59. Kh2 Rg2 ) 
 ( { and } 55... Ng4+ 55. Rxg4 { is hopeless after } Kxg4 { . Final remarks 1. This game was a fine illustration of how a strong knight can dominate a bad bishop. In fact, White’s bishop did not do anything useful throughout the entire game. 2. From this example, the reader should learn to treat the bad bishop as a serious problem and find the first opportunity to trade it or make use of it. In this game, White was probably too careless to realize that 22. Ba3 could secure equality, while 22.Bd2 would lock the bishop inside the pawn chain permanently. 3. From Black’s play, the pawn sacrifice 36...h4 followed by 40...g5 was simply superb, as White cannot survive once the position becomes open. } ) 

 0-1

