 { Sebastien Maze – Ni Hua, Biel 2011 Learning objective: In this game we see how strong Black’s bad bishop can be when it is outside the pawn chain. } 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 c5 6. Be3 Qb6 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Bb5 ( { Another option is the complex } 8. O-O Qxb2 9. Qe1 cxd4 10. Bxd4 Nxd4 11. Nxd4 Bb4 { . } ) 
 { Now Black goes outside the opening book with: } Nh6 $5 ( { More common is } 8... c4 { . } ) 
9. dxc5 ( { If } 9. Bxh6 $2 gxh6 { then Black’s doubled h-pawns are not vulnerable. } 10. O-O Rg8 11. dxc5 Bxc5 $15 { Followed by 12...O-O-O and doubling rooks on the g-file. } ) 
Bxc5 10. Nd4 Bxd4 11. Bxd4 Qc7 12. Bxc6+ Qxc6 { We have reached the structure of interest and, unlike previous examples, Black’s bishop is outside the pawn chain controlling an important diagonal. } ( { Another option was } 12... bxc6 $5 13. Na4 O-O 14. O-O Bg6 15. b3 Nf5 { with a roughly equal position. } ) 
13. O-O ( { PH: } 13. h4 $1 { is much stronger. After } O-O 14. Qe2 Rac8 15. f3 { suddenly Black's pieces on the kingside become very cramped, and the endgame after } Qc4 16. Qxc4 Rxc4 17. O-O-O { is unpleasant for Black. } ) 
Bg6 14. Rc1 { Supporting c2 to release the knight. } ( { If } 14. g4 { to keep the knight out of the game, there is time for } Ng8 15. f4 ( { or } 15. h4 $2 h5 $1 { ∓ } ) 
Ne7 16. Qe2 h5 $1 { ⩱ and note how the knight and bishop work well together to prevent a kingside attack and to find counterplay. } ) 
Nf5 ( { But not } 14... O-O $6 { since after } 15. g4 $1 { the g8-square is occupied, hence the knight is out of the game and there is no time for } Kh8 { to follow with ...Ng8-e7 due to: } 16. h4 $14 ) 
15. Ne2 a5 { Beginning queenside counterplay. } 16. c3 O-O 17. Re1 Rfc8 ( { Interesting was } 17... Rac8 $5 { since later in the game, Black could have used a rook on f8 . } ) 
18. f3 b5 19. Bf2 Qb7 20. g4 Ne7 21. h4 { Not the healthiest kingside attacking method, but there is nothing better. } ( { The standard set-up with f3-f4, } . Nd4 { and f4-f5 is ineffective here, for example: 21.f4 } b4 22. Qd2 bxc3 23. Rxc3 Rxc3 { 24.Nxc3 h5 } 25. h3 { Rc8⩱ } ) 
h5 { The sharpest continuation. } ( { An option was } 21... h6 22. h5 Bh7 23. Qd2 b4 24. Nd4 bxc3 25. Rxc3 Rxc3 26. Qxc3 a4 { with level chances. } ) 
22. Qd2 ( { Better was } 22. gxh5 Bxh5 23. Ng3 Bg6 24. h5 Bh7 25. h6 $1 { forcing open some lines, but after } Nf5 $1 26. Nxf5 Bxf5 27. Qd2 Qe7 { Black is holding well; note again the power of his light-squared bishop. PH: Or even } Kf8 28. hxg7 Kxg7 { , intending Kf8-e8. } ) 
Nc6 { Clearing e7 for the queen. } ( { A better continuation was } 22... b4 $1 { and after } 23. Qg5 ( { or } 22... Nc6 22. Ng3 hxg4 24. fxg4 Bh7 $5 { ∞ } ) 
hxg4 24. fxg4 bxc3 25. Rxc3 Qxb2 $5 { Black can sacrifice a piece to obtain excellent compensation with: } 26. Rxc8 Rxc8 27. Qxe7 Rc4 $1 { Now a possible continuation is } 28. Qd8 Kh7 29. Ng3 Rxg4 30. Qxa5 Be4 31. Re3 Qxe5 { followed by ...f7-f5 with promising chances. } ) 
 ( { PH: Correct would have been } 22... hxg4 23. fxg4 Be4 24. Ng3 Ng6 $1 { and White's kingside is just too loose. } 25. Nxe4 dxe4 { will be followed by ...Rd8 and White is in trouble. } ) 
23. Ng3 ( { Now } 23. Qg5 { is met by } Qe7 $1 24. Qxe7 Nxe7 25. gxh5 Bxh5 26. Kg2 b4 $5 { ⩱ gaining queenside play, while White’s kingside plans are dead due to his inflexible pawn structure. } ) 
hxg4 24. h5 Bh7 25. fxg4 Qe7 26. Qf4 { We have reached a critical moment. The reader should observe this position and note how crucial is the difference between having the light-squared bishop on c8 and having it on h7. In previous examples the light-squared bishop played no role in the defence, while now it is an integral part of Black’s fortress. } b4 27. g5 $2 ( { Much better was: } 27. c4 $1 { And the disadvantage to Black of having a bishop on the h7-b1 diagonal becomes evident. Typically c3-c4 allows } dxc4 { giving a powerful diagonal to the bishop on b7 . But here only White’s pieces will benefit from } dxc4 { as lines open for the rooks and e4 is cleared for the knight. 27...dxc4 } 28. Rxc4 { Black must be precise to avoid problems, for example: } Qd7 $2 ( { Correct is } 28. g5 Qb7 29. h6 Ne7 30. Qg5 Bg6 { with a complicated game where White has a plus. } ) 
 { The text move runs into: } 29. h6 $1 Ne7 30. Rd4 Nd5 31. Qg5 Bg6 32. hxg7 Kxg7 33. Nh5 { And White is winning. } ) 
bxc3 28. bxc3 Rab8 $17 { White has voluntarily accepted this position, and we may ask: ”Why?” Black’s queenside play has resulted in a weak c3-pawn and undisputed control of the b-file (the b1-square is covered by the bishop!) . Meanwhile the centre is open and vulnerable, hence White’s flank attack is unlikely to work. The game continued: } 29. h6 $2 { Increasing White’s problems. } ( { Safer was } 29. Qg4 Bd3 30. h6 g6 31. a4 Rb2 32. Qf4 Rcb8 $17 { though Black is in control. } ) 
gxh6 30. gxh6 Kh8 31. Re2 Rg8 { And now it is Black who will attack on the kingside. White is lost already as his pieces are poorly coordinated, while his pawns are spread as weaknesses across the board. } 32. Kh2 Qg5 33. Be3 Qxf4 34. Bxf4 Rg4 $1 { Now White will fall into an unlikely mating net. } 35. Rf2 Rbg8 36. Kh3 ( { The option } 36. Kg2 Be4 37. Kh3 Ne7 { is similar to the game. } ) 
Ne7 $1 { This is the key; the knight comes to f5 clearing the g-file and supporting ...Rh4. } 37. Rf3 ( 37. Rg1 { doesn’t help after } Nf5 38. Rf3 Rh4 39. Kg2 Rxf4 $1 40. Rxf4 Rxg3 41. Kf2 Rxc3 { . } ) 
Be4 38. h7 Kxh7 ( { White resigns, as he is either losing material or getting mated. } 38... Kxh7 38. Rf2 Nf5 $1 40. Nxf5 Bxf5 { and mate is unstoppable, for example: } 41. Bd2 ( 40... Kxh7 40. Kh2 Rh4 ) 
Rg3 42. Kh2 Rh3 ) 
 ( { Or } 38... Kxh7 38. Nxe4 dxe4 40. Rf2 Nf5 { followed by Rh4#. Final remarks 1. The fact that Black’s bishop was on the h7-b1 diagonal played a key role throughout the game. The main thing to notice is how White could not carry out his standard f4-f5 break because this bishop was ideally placed to prevent it. Instead he had to settle for the far less effective h2-h4-h5 plan which ultimately backfired. 2. Black’s queenside counterplay with ...b5-b4 was also a significant obstacle to White’s attack, forcing him to hurry up in his kingside actions. Black probably should have kept an eye on the possible 27. c4! and in retrospect it could have been better to play 22...b4 opening the queenside at once. 3. If we observe the later stages of the game, we see how Black’s supposedly bad bishop actually combines attack and defence in an excellent fashion. In contrast, White’s bad bishop on f2 really is a bad piece and contributes nothing to the battle. } ) 

