 { Trading pieces and the ...g7-g5 break One of Black’s common strategies in this structure is to head directly for a solid endgame either to secure a draw or to outplay White in a long game. As an example take the variation: } 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 Bxd4 10. Bxd4 Nxd4 11. Qxd4 Qb6 12. Nb5 ( { PH: White can avoid the ending with } 12. Qd2 { , but it allows } Qxb2 { and Black is doing alright in the complications. } ) 
Qxd4 13. Nxd4 Ke7 { It is only move 13 and we have already reached an endgame. In fact, this exact position has occurred 75 times in my database and similar positions arise from many variations. White has more space and Black still needs to solve the problem of his bad bishop. Nevertheless, Black has a solid position with many resources, and even the slightest slip from White will convert the game into full equality. The game Pruijssers – Reinderman, Netherlands (ch) 2013, continued: } 14. Bd3 ( { PH: Given that Black will reroute the knight to c6 and play for ...g5, in retrospect the best move is } 14. h4 $1 Nb8 15. h5 Nc6 16. Nf3 { , when Black still has some issues - the knights remain on the board and there is obviously no ...g5 anymore. } ) 
 ( { Possibly Black has to answer } 14. h4 { with } h5 { , when } 15. c4 { opens the position a bit, and after } dxc4 16. Bxc4 Nb6 17. Bb3 { Black has to watch out for possible invasions on c7 or d6. } ) 
Nb8 $1 15. Kd2 Nc6 { Offering to trade another piece! } 16. Nb3 { Keeping more pieces on the board, but steering away from the centre. } ( { If } 16. c3 Nxd4 17. cxd4 Bd7 { Black’s bad bishop is no problem at all, and after } 18. Rac1 Rac8 19. h4 h5 20. b3 f5 21. exf6 gxf6 { Black experienced no difficulties in making a draw in Guliyev – Andersson, Maarssen 2013. } ) 
 ( { More logical was } 16. Nf3 { though after } Bd7 17. c3 Rac8 { Black is doing fine. } ) 
Bd7 17. Rhf1 { Now Black finds an opportunity for concrete counterplay against White’s centre with: } g5 $1 { Undermining the support of the e5-pawn. } 18. g3 ( { Of course } 18. fxg5 $2 { is completely illogical and after } Rag8 19. Rae1 Rxg5 $17 { Black is winning the central e5-pawn with a big advantage. } ) 
 ( 18. Rae1 { is an option. After } gxf4 19. Rxf4 Rag8 20. g3 Rg5 21. Nd4 $1 { White is just in time to maintain equality since } Nxe5 { is met by } 22. Nf5 $1 exf5 23. Rxe5 Kd6 24. Re3 { with level chances, since the f5-pawn could be lost at any time. } ) 
Rag8 19. Rf2 gxf4 ( { An alternative was } 19... h5 $5 20. Re1 h4 21. c3 hxg3 22. hxg3 Rh3 23. Re3 gxf4 24. gxf4 Rh4 { with pressure against the f4-pawn. } ) 
20. gxf4 Rg7 { Now Black has gained an open g-file while White has to deal with the vulnerable f4- and h2-pawns. The game is approximately balanced though Black’s side is slightly easier to conduct, and indeed Black eventually won the game. } 
